<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!-- The chassis has no notification or battery indicator LED. -->
    <integer name="config_deviceLightCapabilities">0</integer>

    <!-- LiveDisplay has nothing to drive on this hardware.
           dumpsys lineagelivedisplay -> mConfig=capabilities={}
           dumpsys SurfaceFlinger     -> uses color management: 0, ColorMode::NATIVE only
         There is no LineageHardware backend at all: no COLOR_BALANCE, no display
         colour calibration, no outdoor mode, no CABC, no picture adjustment.

         AND THE ONE BACKEND THAT COULD HAVE EXISTED DOES NOT. LineageOS ships
         vendor.lineage.livedisplay@2.0-service-mediatek in
         hardware/mediatek (lineage-17.1), whose DisplayColorCalibration drives
         /sys/devices/platform/mtk_disp_mgr.0/rgb; the matching policy is
         already in this checkout at device/lineage/sepolicy/mediatek/. The
         surrounding evidence all pointed the right way: /dev/mtk_disp_mgr is
         labelled here, CONFIG_MTK_AAL_SUPPORT=y, CONFIG_MTK_PQ_COLOR_MODE is
         "DISP". So it was worth one command. Measured on the handset:

             /sys/devices/platform/mtk_disp_mgr.0  exists
             .../mtk_disp_mgr.0/rgb                does NOT exist
             the directory holds only driver, driver_override, modalias,
             power, subsystem, uevent

         i.e. the platform device is bound and publishes no calibration
         attribute. That HAL would find nothing. Do not clone hardware/mediatek
         for this; re-run the ls if the kernel is ever replaced.

         The colour-temperature half is now served by AOSP instead. The device
         overlay sets config_nightDisplayAvailable=true (see the measurement in
         overlay/frameworks/base/core/res/res/values/config.xml), so Settings'
         Display menu carries AOSP's own Night Light entry, which
         display_settings.xml:44 lists alongside this one. LiveDisplay itself
         would defer to it anyway: ColorTemperatureController.java:84 computes
         mUseTemperatureAdjustment = !mNightDisplayAvailable AND (...), so with
         Night Light available LiveDisplay drives no temperature of its own.

         An earlier revision of this comment justified disabling LiveDisplay with
         "Night display: Not available". That was circular, because Night Display
         was unavailable only for the reason that this tree had never overridden
         the bool, and it is why the handset shipped with no blue-light filter of
         any kind.

         Settings gates the whole LiveDisplay entry on this bool alone, not on
         capabilities (res/xml/display_settings.xml). With capabilities empty AND
         night display unavailable, LiveDisplaySettings strips the colour
         profile, outdoor mode, reading mode, CABC, colour enhancement and
         picture adjustment preferences - but the block that would also remove
         the mode picker and the colour-temperature slider is itself guarded by
         isNightDisplayAvailable, so it never runs. The result is a Display menu
         entry that opens a screen with two controls that silently do nothing:
         LiveDisplayService.setMode() returns false without hasModeSupport().

         The related defaults config_defaultAutoOutdoorMode / config_defaultCABC /
         config_defaultColorEnhancement are deliberately NOT overridden - their
         controllers return immediately without the matching LineageHardware
         feature, so they are already inert. This one bool is the correct fix. -->
    <bool name="config_enableLiveDisplay">false</bool>

    <!-- 720x1560 is 2.167:1, past the 1.86 ratio at which Android 10
         letterboxes non-resizeable apps that target below O
         (ActivityRecord.computeBounds). This bool gates LineageOS's per-app
         "Long screen" override (lineage-sdk LongScreen.java, surfaced by
         Settings' display_settings.xml, enforced in ActivityRecord). With it
         false the user cannot opt any app out of the black bars. The override
         is opt-in per application, so enabling it changes nothing until the
         user asks for it. -->
    <bool name="config_haveHigherAspectRatioScreen">true</bool>

    <!-- config_haveNotch stays FALSE, and this is a trap worth naming: the
         device does have a punch hole, so the name reads like a missing
         override. Its only consumer in 17.1 is LineageParts' StatusBarSettings,
         which uses it to REMOVE the network-traffic preference. SystemUI
         already reserves the aperture through cutout_space_view, and the fill
         layer is live on the handset (ColorLayer "Letterbox - top" crop
         [0,0,720,70]). Setting it true would delete a working feature and fix
         nothing. -->

    <!-- config_deviceHardwareKeys and config_deviceHardwareWakeKeys stay at the
         lineage-sdk default of 64 (volume rocker only, no capacitive keys), and
         both bits are correct.

         Do NOT clear the wake bit. Earlier revisions of this project recorded
         that "matrix keys cannot wake this SoC at any configuration", on the
         reasoning that MTK's kpd_pdrv_suspend() writes KP_EN=0 so the keypad
         block cannot assert its SPM wake source. The premise was right and the
         conclusion was wrong: the block is only disabled BECAUSE of that write,
         and rootdir/etc/init/hw/init.mt6755.rc now sets
         /sys/bus/platform/drivers/mtk-kpd/kpd_call_state to 2, which makes both
         the suspend and resume hooks skip it. KP_EN stays 1 across suspend.

         Confirmed on the handset, end to end:
           * nine suspend/resume cycles logged the call_status == 2 branch and
             never "KEYPAD is disabled" (E-028)
           * R12_KP_IRQ_B is bit 2 of the SPM R12 wake mask
             (spm_v2/mt_spm_internal.c wakesrc_str[]), and the live suspend mask
             0x1e86c5e7 has it set
           * the owner pressed Volume Up with the screen off and the handset
             woke
         So "volume buttons wake device" in Settings works on this build.

         The bit is load-bearing for a second reason as well: it also gates
         mVolumeAnswerCall in PhoneWindowManager.updateSettings()
         (frameworks/base .../policy/PhoneWindowManager.java:2451), while
         LineageParts hides that toggle on a different condition entirely -
         hasVolumeKeys plus voice capability (ButtonSettings:371). Clearing the
         wake bit would silently kill answer-on-volume while leaving its switch
         on screen. -->
</resources>
